# Who a commit or pull request may name as its author, committer or
# co-author, and what it may never carry. Read by
# installer/git-hooks/no-agent-attribution (setup installs both into
# ~/.config/agent-kit/git-hooks) and meant for any CI guard that wants the
# same rule. The owner's decision: the owner's own identity, plus GitHub App
# bots that are not AI agents. Every AI agent and model provider is refused.
#
# One rule per line, "<kind> <value>"; blank lines and # comments are skipped.
# Everything is compared in lower case.
#
#   name <full name>        an allowed human name; exact match
#   email <address>         an allowed human email; exact match
#                           A human identity is allowed when both its name
#                           and its email are listed.
#   bot <ERE>               the email of a GitHub App bot. Allowed unless its
#                           name or its login matches an agent-bot pattern.
#   agent-bot <ERE>         a bot name or login that is an AI agent: refused
#   automation <name>       a non-AI automation that commits under its own
#                           name (flux, a worker): allowed as a commit's
#                           author or committer, never as a co-author, and
#                           only when its email matches no refuse pattern
#   committer <address>     an extra committer email (GitHub's own, for a
#                           commit merged or edited on github.com)
#   refuse <ERE>            any message line that matches is refused
#
# A per-machine file, ~/.config/agent-kit/allowed-identities.local, may add
# name and email lines (a work identity, say). Other kinds there are ignored.

